x^2+4y^2+3x-12y-64&=(x^2+3x)+(4y^2-12y)-64\\
&=x(x+3)+4y(y-3)-64\\
&=(x+3)(x+4y)-64
\end{align*}The first two terms have a common factor of $x+3$, so we can write \begin{align*}
(x+3)(x+4y)-64&=(x+3)(x+4y-64/ (x+3))\\
&=(x+3)(x+4y-64/(x+3))
\end{align*}We can not factor this expression any further, so the answer is $\boxed{no}$.

