\begin{align*}
2,4,6,8,10,\cdots,96,98,100
\end{align*}
We notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of $2$.  The first term is $2$ and the last term is $100$.  We can find the number of terms in this sequence by using the formula for the nth term of an arithmetic sequence:
$$a_n=a_1+(n-1)d$$
where $a_n$ is the nth term, $a_1$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $n$ is the number of terms.  We have that
\begin{align*}
a_n&=100\\
a_1&=2\\
d&=2
\end{align*}
Plugging in these values into the formula, we have
\begin{align*}
100&=2+(n-1)2\\
\Rightarrow\qquad 2(n-1)&=98\\
\Rightarrow\qquad n-1&=49\\
\Rightarrow\qquad n&=\boxed{50}
